## 1. Methodology: SBTDD + Document Hierarchy

This project follows the SBTDD paradigm
(Spec + Behavior + Test Driven Development).

### 1.1 Document hierarchy

| File | Purpose | Tracked |
|------|---------|---------|
| `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` | Global code rules — maximum precedence | Global, outside repo |
| `CLAUDE.md` | Developer-local permanent memory: lasting architecture decisions, accumulated context (NOT runtime state — see §2) | **No** — gitignored |
| `CLAUDE.local.md` | Project rules and standards (immutable during execution, see §2.4) | **No** — gitignored |
| `.claude/` (entire directory) | Agent configuration: hooks, permissions, state file, TDD-Guard internal data | **No** — gitignored |
| `sbtdd/spec-behavior-base.md` | Base spec pre-brainstorming (input to `/brainstorming`) | **No** — gitignored |
| `sbtdd/spec-behavior.md` | SDD + BDD specification of the active feature (output of `/brainstorming`) | **No** — gitignored |
| `planning/claude-plan-tdd.md` | Active TDD plan, reviewed and approved | **No** — gitignored |
| `planning/claude-plan-tdd-org.md` | Original TDD plan generated by `/writing-plans` (pre-MAGI reference) | **No** — gitignored |

**Tracking policy:**

`CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.claude/`, `sbtdd/`, and `planning/` are all
**gitignored**. The entire SBTDD process is **developer-local**; git records
source code and TDD-cycle commits only. There is no shared team coordination
via these artifacts — each developer instantiates their own local configuration
from the template and configures their own hooks. If lasting architecture
decisions need to be shared, commit them to project documentation (README,
docs/), not to `CLAUDE.md`.

Implications:
- A new developer cloning the repo **instantiates their own `CLAUDE.local.md`**
  from the template and **configures their own `.claude/settings.json`** with
  the hooks in §4.1.
- The state file (`.claude/session-state.json`) is also local — it is
  **created on first run and resumed thereafter — never overwritten mid-plan**
  (see §2.3).

